How Spring Boot works with MinIO

A typical upload flow:

Client
   |
POST /upload
   |
Spring Boot
   |
MinioClient.putObject()
   |
MinIO

Download flow:

Client
   |
GET /download/123
   |
Spring Boot
   |
MinioClient.getObject()
   |
MinIO



Example code (Spring Boot)

Upload:

minioClient.putObject(
    PutObjectArgs.builder()
        .bucket("documents")
        .object("resume.pdf")
        .stream(inputStream, fileSize, -1)
        .contentType("application/pdf")
        .build()
);

Download:

InputStream inputStream =
    minioClient.getObject(
        GetObjectArgs.builder()
            .bucket("documents")
            .object("resume.pdf")
            .build()
    );

Delete:

minioClient.removeObject(
    RemoveObjectArgs.builder()
        .bucket("documents")
        .object("resume.pdf")
        .build()
);
MinIO vs MySQL
| Feature         | MySQL                     | MinIO             |
| --------------- | ------------------------- | ----------------- |
| Structured data | ✅ Excellent               | ❌ No              |
| Images          | ⚠️ Possible but not ideal | ✅ Excellent       |
| Videos          | ❌ Poor                    | ✅ Excellent       |
| Large files     | ❌ Not ideal               | ✅ Designed for it |
| Backups         | ❌ Heavy                   | ✅ Efficient       |
| Scalability     | Medium                    | High              |

MinIO vs Amazon S3
| Feature                 | MinIO                                            | Amazon S3                 |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| Runs on your own server | ✅                                                | ❌                         |
| Cloud service           | Optional                                         | ✅                         |
| S3-compatible API       | ✅                                                | ✅                         |
| Cost                    | Free (self-hosted)                               | Pay-as-you-go             |
| Best for                | Private infrastructure, on-premises, development | Cloud-native applications |

Interview answer (1 minute)

MinIO is an open-source, S3-compatible object storage system used to store unstructured data such as images, videos, PDFs, backups, and logs.
Instead of storing large files in a relational database, applications upload them to MinIO and store only the file reference in the database.
This improves performance, reduces database size, and makes file storage more scalable.
Since MinIO is compatible with the Amazon S3 API, applications can often switch between MinIO and S3 with minimal code changes.